---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
---
<ToolLayout
  tool="seed"
  units={false}
  intro="Seed packets say things like “sow indoors 6–8 weeks before the last frost”. Enter your own reference date and the timings from the packet to get real calendar dates and a printable schedule."
  related={[
    { id: 'spacing', why: 'see how many of those seedlings will fit in the bed.' },
    { id: 'soil', why: 'work out soil for the beds they are going into.' },
    { id: 'mulch', why: 'estimate mulch for after planting out.' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Enter a reference date.</strong> This is the date the packet counts from. It is usually your local average last spring frost date, but it can be a planned transplant date, a first autumn frost date, or anything else. You choose it; no location is needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Say what the date is</strong>, so the schedule reads correctly. If some packets count from a different date, such as your first autumn frost, add it as a second reference date (up to three).</li>
    <li><strong>Add a line for each timing on the packet.</strong> Enter the number, or both ends of a range such as 6 and 8, choose weeks or days, and choose before or after.</li>
    <li><strong>Label the task</strong> as sow indoors, transplant outdoors or direct sow, and add the crop name if you like. With more than one reference date, choose which one each line is counted from.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    One crop can have several lines. Tomatoes might have one line for sowing indoors and another for transplanting.
  </p>

  <h3>Succession sowing</h3>
  <p>
    For crops you sow little and often, enter the number of sowings and the days between them. Four sowings 14 days apart adds three more dates, 14, 28 and 42 days after the first. Use the interval your seed packet or your own experience suggests.
  </p>

  <h3>Where to find a frost date</h3>
  <p>
    Prunest does not look up or estimate frost dates. A national weather service, a local extension office or gardening society, or an experienced neighbour are better sources than a calculator. Whatever date you use is an average or an estimate, and frost can arrive later than expected.
  </p>

  <h2>How the dates are calculated</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A week is exactly 7 days, and offsets are whole days or whole weeks.</li>
    <li>“Before” counts back from the reference date and “after” counts forward.</li>
    <li>A range gives a window with an earliest and a latest date. If you type a range the other way round, such as 8 to 6, you get the same window.</li>
    <li>Dates are handled as plain calendar dates with no time of day, so your timezone and daylight saving changes cannot move a result by a day. Leap years and year ends are handled by the calendar.</li>
  </ul>

  <Fragment slot="more">
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <div class="worked">
      <p><strong>A gardener uses 10 May 2027 as their last spring frost date. The tomato packet says “sow indoors 6–8 weeks before last frost”.</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>6 weeks is 42 days. Counting back from 10 May: 10 days takes you to 30 April, 30 more to 31 March, and 2 more to <strong>29 March 2027</strong>.</li>
        <li>8 weeks is 56 days, 14 days earlier again: <strong>15 March 2027</strong>.</li>
        <li>The sowing window is <strong>15 to 29 March 2027</strong>.</li>
        <li>The same packet says “transplant 2 weeks after last frost”: 10 May + 14 days = <strong>24 May 2027</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This shows the arithmetic only. The dates and timings are an illustration, not a planting recommendation for any region. “Try an example” loads similar lines using the next 10 May, plus an autumn line counted from a first autumn frost date of 10 October.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What this calculator does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No frost dates or locations.</strong> You supply the reference date.</li>
      <li><strong>No guarantee of safe planting.</strong> A calculated date is arithmetic. It cannot tell you that frost has passed or that the soil is warm enough.</li>
      <li><strong>No crop timings.</strong> There is no plant database. Offsets come from your seed packets.</li>
      <li><strong>Up to three reference dates per schedule.</strong> That covers spring and autumn sowing in one plan; for more, build a second schedule.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Timeline</h2>
    <p>
      The timeline draws every task as a bar against the months, with a dashed line at your reference date, so you can see at a glance what happens before and after it. The table beside it has the exact dates.
    </p>

    <h2>Calendar file</h2>
    <p>
      “Download calendar file” saves an .ics file made in your browser, with one all-day event per line (a window becomes a multi-day event). Most calendar apps can import it. Nothing is sent to Prunest.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
<script>
  import '../tools/seed';
</script>
